<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">written between 16 and 19 July <a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Mazurek Dąbrowskiego</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In 1795 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> was partitioned for the third time by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and erased from the map; the partitioning monarchs pledged never to use the name &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>&quot; in their titles. Many, even leaders of the Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, took it as the end of the nation: Hugo Kołłątaj wrote that Poland &quot;no longer belonged to currently extant nations&quot;.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/poland-is-not-yet-lost.jpg" alt="Poland Is Not Yet Lost" width="330" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jozef Wybicki and Kazimierz Sikorski · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polish_national_anthem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over the following centuries it gave way to newer patriotic songs, notably &quot;Rota&quot; and &quot;<b>Poland Is Not Yet Lost</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bogurodzica">Bogurodzica (Mother of God)</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars think it was also influenced by the Polish patriotic song &quot;<b>Poland Is Not Yet Lost</b>&quot; (1797), popular among the peoples of the former Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth then struggling for independence; the January Uprising began a few months later.</blockquote><a href="/songs/national-anthem-of-ukraine">State Anthem of Ukraine</a></li><li><blockquote>…1922 it was the state anthem of the Republic of Central Lithuania, a puppet state wholly dependent on interwar Poland, and in Poland itself it was among the songs considered for the national anthem, favoured especially by the political right; &quot;<b>Poland Is Not Yet Lost</b>&quot; was chosen instead.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rota">Rota</a></li><li><blockquote>He arranged it for the Royal Siamese Navy Orchestra, in a tune vaguely like Poland's anthem &quot;<b>Poland Is Not Yet Lost</b>&quot;, and asked that his name be kept secret.</blockquote><a href="/songs/thai-national-anthem">Thai National Anthem</a></li></ul></section>
